    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "TacZh",
      "x": 1520,
      "y": 8600,
      "name": "NOTE · Landing page image provenance",
      "width": 460,
      "height": 474,
      "content": "IMAGE PROVENANCE — READ BEFORE ANY PUBLIC USE\n\nLOGO (supplied by client):\n  shats-logo.png        dark teal, for light backgrounds\n  shats-logo-light.png  lightened, for dark backgrounds\n  Cropped to artwork bounds from 'shats 1.png'. The dark\n  original is near-invisible on the teal/night surfaces,\n  which is why the light variant exists — use the right\n  one for the background, do not recolour on the fly.\n\nAI-GENERATED (placeholders, not real people):\n  • S5 \"A trained neighbour\" — health worker taking BP\n  • S6 portrait labelled Dr. Chidi Okonkwo\n  • S8 \"Headaches, stress and your brain\"\n  • S8 \"Sickle cell\" — mother and child\n  Files: design/images/generated-*.png\n\nThese faces are synthetic. Do NOT present them as real\npractitioners or real patients. Replace with commissioned\nor licensed photography, and for any doctor shown, use a\nreal registered doctor with their written consent.\n\nUNSPLASH STOCK (check licence for commercial use):\n  • S6 portraits for Dr. Amina Bello and Dr. Tunde Adeyemi\n  • S8 \"Taking your medicine the right way\"\n  • Hero background — clinic.jpg (client supplied)\n\nSTATISTICS in S2 verified Aug 2026:\n  3.9 doctors per 10,000 — WHO threshold is 10. The often\n  quoted \"WHO recommends 1:600\" does not exist; Africa\n  Check traced it to no WHO source.\n  45% rural — World Bank, 2024\n  28.7% of global maternal deaths — WHO MMEIG, 2023"
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "CBIJV",
      "x": 1520,
      "y": 9080,
      "name": "NOTE · Landing page motion spec",
      "width": 460,
      "height": 940,
      "content": "MOTION SPEC — landing page\nReference: impilo.health, its HowItWorks section\n\nPencil is static, so the motion lives in the Next app:\n  shats/app/page.tsx\n  shats/components/  CoverVeil, OverlayStack,\n                     JourneyPanel, ScrollFx, Reveal\n\ndesign/landing.html is the browsable preview of THIS\ncanvas. Rebuild it after any re-export from Pencil:\n  Export the L0 frame as html-css, layer names on\n  python3 design/build-preview.py <export> design/landing.html\nNothing in landing.html is hand-edited, so canvas,\npreview and Next app all agree.\n\nFIVE MOVES, IN ORDER\n\n1 · HERO OVERLAY   Hero + S2 · The Distance\n  The hero pins and S2 rides up over it, sinking it to\n  65% black. Both frames are one viewport tall (860), so\n  the pin releases the moment the cover completes.\n  This overlay ends at S2. The journey does not stack.\n\n2 · JOURNEY PIN AND HOLD   Step 01 to Step 04\n  Each step pins, holds about 0.78 of a screen, then\n  releases and clears out as the next arrives. Panels\n  never overlap. Inside them the contents unblur and\n  grow into place and back out again, and the visual\n  fades a beat later. Same unblur-and-grow as move 3.\n  See NOTE · Journey pin and hold.\n\n3 · UNBLUR AND GROW   S4 · Works Without\n  Arrives blurred and slightly small, sharpens and grows\n  into place, and reverses on the way back up. It is\n  only type and icons, so a slide would look busy.\n\n4 · SECOND OVERLAY   S4 to S7\n  The overlay resumes here. S4, S5, S6 and S7 stack the\n  way the hero does: each pins and the next covers it.\n  All four are one viewport tall for that reason — a\n  short panel would let the one beneath show through.\n  S7 · Emergency is deliberately STATIC. It is the one\n  moment on the page that should read as an alarm\n  rather than as a flourish, and it ends the run.\n\n5 · TAIL FADES   S8 to S11\n  Each block inside these fades in and back out on\n  scroll position, in a soft cascade rather than the\n  whole screen at once.\n\nEVERYTHING FROM 2 DOWN IS SCROLL-POSITION DRIVEN, not\ntriggered. Each value is a function of where the element\nsits, so all of it runs backwards when you scroll up and\nnothing latches on first sight.\n\nOpacity and blur are always applied to a wrapper around a\nsection's contents, never to the section — fading the\nframe itself would fade its own background and show the\npage straight through.\n\nAll motion is off under prefers-reduced-motion, and off\nbelow 1024px where every panel is an ordinary block."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "AAqwj",
      "x": 1520,
      "y": 10680,
      "name": "NOTE · Journey pin and hold",
      "width": 460,
      "height": 900,
      "content": "PIN AND HOLD — Step 01 to Step 04\n\nEach of these four frames is one full screen, 1440 x 860.\nThat is deliberate: one step = one screen.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS ON SCROLL\n  1. the step slides up into place\n  2. it PINS and holds still while you keep scrolling\n  3. it releases and scrolls away upward\n  4. the next step arrives from below and repeats\n\nThe panels never overlap each other, and there is no\ndivider between them — the hand-off is carried by the\ndissolve below, not by a rule.\n\nTHE DISSOLVE (this is why the panels look empty here)\nPencil can only draw the held state. On the real page\nthe contents of each panel are tied to scroll position,\nnot to a one-shot trigger, so they run backwards when\nyou scroll back up:\n\n  Step NN Text     UNBLURS AND GROWS. The number, the\n                   heading and the body move together as\n                   one block: blurred and slightly small\n                   as the panel rises, sharp and full\n                   size while it is pinned, then back to\n                   blurred and small as it leaves.\n                   blur 9px to 0, scale 0.93 to 1.\n                   Scaled from the left edge so growing\n                   does not drag the text sideways.\n                   Same treatment as S4 · Works Without.\n\n  Step NN Visual   pure fade, no movement. Starts later\n                   and finishes later than the text, so\n                   the words land first and the card\n                   settles behind them.\n\nBecause it is a function of scroll position, every state\nis reachable in both directions. Nothing latches.\n\nHOW THE PIN IS BUILT\nposition:sticky top:0 on the panel, inside a wrapper\n178svh tall. The 78% surplus height is the hold.\nimpilo.health gets the same result with GSAP\nScrollTrigger pin — no library is needed for it.\n\nSee NOTE · Landing page motion spec for the hero\noverlay that runs before this section.\n\nWIDTHS — THIS CANVAS IS ONE SIZE, THE APP IS NOT\nThis artboard is 1440 wide, so it cannot show what the\nsection does on a larger screen. Held to these exact\npixel widths the content strands itself in the middle of\na wide display with dead margin either side.\n\nIn the app the journey scales with the viewport:\n  container   min(1920px, 92vw)\n  gap         clamp(48px, 4vw, 80px)\n  text block  clamp(430px, 34vw, 680px)\n  card        clamp(440px, 36vw, 760px)\n\nAt 1440 that resolves to text 490 and card 482, which is\nwhat this canvas draws (472 / 520). Above about 1900 the\ncaps take over — 680 and 760 — because longer measures\nstop being readable, so extra width becomes margin by\ndesign rather than by accident."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "C7V11i",
      "x": 1520,
      "y": 14119,
      "name": "NOTE · Second overlay run",
      "width": 460,
      "height": 600,
      "content": "SECOND OVERLAY RUN — S4 to S7\n\nThese four frames stack. Each pins to the top of the\nscreen and the next one rides up and covers it, exactly\nlike the hero at the top of the page.\n\nWHY THEY ARE ALL 860 TALL\nA panel shorter than the screen cannot cover the one\nbeneath it, so the previous section stays visible under\nthe incoming one and the whole effect breaks. Every\nframe in an overlay run has to be one full viewport.\nS6 · The Doctors was 997 and was tightened to fit:\npadding 104 to 56, gap 56 to 34.\n\nPER-SECTION BEHAVIOUR\n  S4 · Works Without   unblurs and grows into place,\n                       reverses on the way back up\n  S5 · The Health Worker   covered by S6\n  S6 · The Doctors     covered by S7\n  S7 · Emergency       STATIC. No animation at all.\n                       It ends the run, and it is the\n                       one moment that should read as\n                       an alarm, not a flourish.\n\nBecause S7 is the last panel and the run container ends\nwhere S7 ends, S7 can never actually pin — which is what\nlets S8 and everything below scroll on normally.\n\nBelow this run, S8 to S11 fade in and back out block by\nblock. See NOTE · Landing page motion spec."
    }
  ],
